<TopicPage slug="subjects/naval-architecture/foundational/floating-things-steadily-hydrostatics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Hold a solid steel ball bearing in one hand and try to imagine it floating. It will not —
    steel is nearly eight times denser than water. Now consider that a modern container ship is a
    quarter-million tonnes of steel, welded boxes of it, and it floats with metres of freeboard
    while carrying another couple of hundred thousand tonnes of cargo. Nothing about that is
    mysterious once you see the trick: the ship is not a lump of steel, it is a <em>steel
    envelope full of air</em>, and its <em>average</em> density — steel, cargo, fuel, crew and
    all that air, divided over its whole volume — is deliberately kept below the density of
    seawater. A ship is a submarine that has decided to spend its life on the surface.
  </p>
  <p>
    Getting a hull to float is the easy half of the problem. The genuinely naval-architectural
    half is <em>upright</em>: a floating body is a body in equilibrium, and the sea is a
    relentless perturbation engine that pushes, rolls and heaves it thousands of times a day.
    Every ship must return from each of those pushes upright, and it must do so in a way that is
    neither too limp (it capsizes) nor too stiff (it rolls so violently that cargo breaks loose
    and crews collapse with seasickness). Hydrostatics is the science of that balance: buoyancy
    holding the ship up, the geometry of the underwater hull holding it steady, and — when both
    are stretched to their legal limits — the load line on the bow telling the master exactly how
    deep this ship may be loaded.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are the first principles of the entire profession. Before resistance, propulsion,
    structures or seakeeping can be designed, the hull must float at the right draft, carry its
    payload, and prove to a regulator that it will come back from a specified list, heel and
    wind gust. Everything else in naval architecture is built on the concepts of this chapter —
    and on a century of tragic lessons about what happens when they are ignored.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Archimedes, B and G, GM and the righting curve" widget="equationStep" config={stabilityStepsCfg} />

  <h2>Buoyancy and displacement</h2>
  <p>
    Archimedes' principle states that the upward buoyant force on a body equals the weight of the
    fluid it displaces. For a floating body, force balance gives the master equation of the field:
    <strong>a floating object displaces exactly its own weight of water</strong>. Load a ship
    heavier, and it must sink deeper until the extra underwater volume makes up the weight; load
    it lighter, and it rises. This is why ships measure themselves in <em>displacement</em>: a
    vessel of 100,000 tonnes displacement occupies, at any instant, precisely 100,000 tonnes of
    seawater. Seawater weighs about 1.025 tonnes per cubic metre, so that hull's underwater
    volume is 100,000 ÷ 1.025 ≈ 97,560 cubic metres — and that number, set against the hull's
    shape, fixes the draft: how deep the waterline sits on the bow.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same law runs in reverse as a design tool. Naval architects divide the ship's weight into
    <strong>lightship</strong> (the empty vessel: steel, machinery, outfit) and
    <strong>deadweight</strong> (everything it carries: cargo, fuel, fresh water, ballast,
    stores, crew). Displacement = lightship + deadweight, and the entire art of cargo ships lies
    in maximising deadweight per tonne of steel. It also gives the workaday quantity
    <strong>TPC — tonnes per centimetre immersion</strong>: for a hull whose waterplane area is
    A square metres, each centimetre of added draft swallows A × 0.01 × 1.025 tonnes of water
    weight. A large bulk carrier with a 6,000 m² waterplane runs about 60 tonnes per centimetre,
    which is why loading crews think in centimetres of draft the way accountants think in
    dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hull fullness is captured by the <strong>block coefficient</strong> C<sub>B</sub> — actual
    underwater volume divided by the box L × B × T (length, beam, draft). A slow, full tanker
    has C<sub>B</sub> around 0.8: its underwater body nearly fills its bounding box. A fast
    warship has C<sub>B</sub> near 0.45: long, slim, pointed. The coefficient links displacement
    to dimensions in one step and quietly encodes the ship's speed intent — no propeller pushes
    a brick efficiently, no tanker is wasted on racing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water density matters more than most passengers realise. The same ship floats
    <em>deeper</em> in fresh water (1.000 t/m³) than in salt (1.025 t/m³), because fresh water
    offers less buoyancy per litre. A vessel that leaves a Baltic or Great Lakes port at its
    marks rises a few centimetres on reaching the open sea; the load-line marks account for
    this, with separate fresh-water allowances. Density, draft and displacement are a
    three-way sliding scale, and the naval architect keeps all three books open at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "heavy things sink and light things float" is the
    kindergarten version, and it fails exactly at ships. A 200,000-tonne tanker floats; a 30-gram
    steel coin sinks. The question is never absolute weight — it is whether the body's average
    density is above or below the fluid's. That is also why a cargo ship that takes on water and
    floods its compartments dies: each flooded space trades air for seawater, raising the average
    density toward the fatal side of 1.025.
  </p>

  <h2>Stability and metacentres</h2>
  <p>
    Two imaginary points govern everything. <strong>G</strong>, the centre of gravity, is where
    the ship's whole weight may be treated as acting; it moves when cargo, fuel or ice moves.
    <strong>B</strong>, the centre of buoyancy, is the centroid of the underwater volume — the
    point where the total upward push acts. Upright and undisturbed, G sits directly above B and
    the two cancel in a straight line. Now let a wave heel the ship a few degrees. The underwater
    wedge that was on the high side emerges, a new wedge submerges on the low side, and B slides
    <em>sideways toward the submerged side</em>. Gravity still pulls straight down through G. Two
    forces, equal and opposite, now acting along lines that no longer align: they form a
    <strong>couple</strong>. Which way the couple turns decides the ship's fate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>metacentre M</strong> is the point where the tilted line of buoyancy crosses the
    ship's original vertical centreline, and the <strong>metacentric height GM</strong> is the
    distance from G up to M. For small heel angles the righting arm — the horizontal offset
    between the two force lines, called <strong>GZ</strong> — is closely approximated by
    GZ ≈ GM·sin θ. If M lies above G, every small heel creates a couple that rotates the ship
    back upright: stable. If the ship is loaded so high that M drops below G, the couple rotates
    it <em>away</em> from upright, and it either settles at a list or rolls over. GM is the
    single most watched number in ship operations, and it can be measured at sea: in an
    <strong>inclining experiment</strong>, a known weight is moved a known distance across the
    deck, the resulting heel angle is read from plumb lines, and GM = w·d ⁄ (Δ·tan θ). Move
    10 tonnes across 10 metres on a 10,000-tonne ship and observe 0.25° of heel, and the ship's
    GM is 100 ⁄ (10,000 × 0.00436) ≈ 2.3 metres. No computer model required — the ocean itself
    signs off.
  </p>
  <p>
    GM compresses a hull's personality into one number. Large GM — wide beam, low weights —
    makes a <strong>stiff</strong> ship: it snaps back upright hard and fast, with a short roll
    period, punishing cargo lashings and stomachs. Small GM makes a <strong>tender</strong>
    ship: gentle, slow rolls, but little reserve against listing forces. Designers aim in
    between; large passenger ships commonly run GM around a metre or so precisely for comfort,
    while loaded bulk carriers and small fishing vessels, exposed to far greater listing risks,
    keep more. The sliders below let you feel the trade-off: righting arm GZ grows with both GM
    and heel, and beam feeds the waterplane geometry that raises M in the first place.
  </p>
  <p>
    The nastiest operational hazard here is the <strong>free-surface effect</strong>. A tank
    that is half-full of fuel, water or fish-hold flood behaves like a liquid pendulum: as the
    ship heels, the liquid runs to the low side and its surface stays level, shifting the
    liquid's centre of gravity outward and — because G is the weighted average of everything —
    dragging the ship's G with it. The result is a <em>virtual rise of G</em>, a reduction in GM
    that occurs without a single gram of cargo moving, and it can erase the entire designed
    stability margin. The physics has a cruel quantitative edge: the correction scales with the
    <em>area</em> of the free surface, not the volume of liquid. Divide a tank's width with
    baffles into three compartments and the free-surface moment falls by a factor of nine. This
    is why tank cleaning schedules, ballast sequences and "sounding logs" are stability
    documents, and why half-full tanks have capsized ferries and fishing vessels whose paper
    stability looked acceptable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> bigger GM is not automatically safer. Beyond comfort and
    cargo security, real capsize resistance is described by the whole <strong>righting-arm
    curve</strong> — GZ across heel angles from upright to the <strong>range of positive
    stability</strong>, where the curve returns to zero. A ship can have a healthy GM and still
    capsize if wind, waves and shifted cargo push it past its curve's maximum. Conversely, ships
    with modest GM but generous reserve buoyancy above deck have survived extraordinary
    abuse. GM is the first chapter of stability, not the whole book.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="righting arm vs heel, beam and GM" widget="sliderLab" config={stabilityCfg} />

  <h2>Load lines and freeboard</h2>
  <p>
    The third pillar is the line painted on the hull of almost every commercial ship in the
    world: the <strong>load line mark</strong>, the circle-and-cross of the <strong>Plimsoll
    line</strong>. Its history is social as much as technical. In Victorian Britain, heavily
    insured, overloaded "coffin ships" sank with routine loss of life while their owners
    profited; the parliamentarian Samuel Plimsoll crusaded against the practice until the
    Merchant Shipping Act of 1876 obliged British ships to carry a mark showing the deepest
    legal loading. The idea — a statutory limit on draft, hence a statutory minimum of
    <strong>freeboard</strong>, the height of the deck above the waterline — was globalised by
    the International Load Line Convention of 1966 and remains in force under the IMO today.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mark is not one line but a comb of them, because the safe depth of loading depends on
    the water the ship will meet. A loaded ship with less freeboard has less <strong>reserve
    buoyancy</strong> — the sealed volume above the waterline that can flood without sinking
    the ship — and less reserve means less margin against boarding seas and damaged stability.
    Winter North Atlantic seas demand more freeboard than a tropical run; fresh water, being
    lighter, lets the same weight sink deeper, so fresh-water marks sit below the salt ones.
    The assigned freeboard is computed for each ship from its length, hull type, sheer,
    superstructure and enclosed openings, and enforcement is a survey-and-certificate matter:
    load past the mark and the port state can detain the ship. For the naval architect the load
    line is where hydrostatics becomes law — the point where buoyancy arithmetic, reserve
    buoyancy and stability margins are summarised in paint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Freeboard and stability interlock in both directions. Deeper loading (smaller freeboard)
    lowers G as cargo goes low and increases the beam at the waterline, usually improving GM —
    but it steals reserve buoyancy and reduces the heel angle at which the deck edge goes
    under. Shallow loading does the reverse. A master loading a ship is therefore not filling a
    tank to a mark; they are negotiating three curves — displacement, GM, freeboard — against a
    voyage's weather, and the stability booklet in the wheelhouse is the map for that
    negotiation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The metacentre has its own geometry.</strong> For small heels, the height of M above
    B is the metacentric radius BM = I ⁄ V, where I is the second moment of the waterplane area
    about the centreline and V the displaced volume. This one formula explains every rule of
    thumb in the trade: beam enters I as the third power, which is why beam is the stability
    lever of choice; and why a ship at light draft (small V, full waterplane) is often
    surprisingly stiff, while a deeply loaded ship grows tender.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Regulations read the whole curve.</strong> Modern intact-stability codes specify
    minima not only for GM but for the righting-arm curve: minimum GZ at 30°, a maximum beyond
    25°, and minimum <em>area</em> under the curve up to given angles — areas being energy, the
    dynamic work waves and wind must do to capsize the ship. The IMO weather criterion adds a
    wind-heeling gust applied to a rolling ship. Damage stability (SOLAS subdivision) asks the
    harder question: with specified compartments flooded, does the ship still float with
    adequate residual stability? Modern passenger ships are increasingly held to the
    two-compartment standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Failure modes the textbooks warn about.</strong> A ship with GM near zero is in
    <em>neutral</em> equilibrium and can settle at an <strong>angle of loll</strong> — floating
    flopped to either side, uprightable but deeply wrong. Ice accretion on rigging and
    superstructure raises G and adds free surface of water; it has sunk northern fishing fleets.
    <strong>Parametric rolling</strong> — a modern container-ship phenomenon in which the hull's
    wave-induced changes in shape synchronise with the roll, pumping energy in over many cycles
    — capsizes the simple GM picture and is handled in seakeeping analysis. And
    <strong>downflooding</strong> sets the absolute limit: the heel at which a non-watertight
    opening (a vent, a door) goes under, and the reserve buoyancy starts draining. Every one of
    these is the elementary balance of this chapter, stressed into a regime Archimedes never
    drew.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The deep frontier is dynamic: hydrostatics assumes the sea
    is slow; hydrodynamics asks what happens when it is not — slamming, green water on deck,
    broaching in following seas. Probabilistic damage-stability rules now treat flooding as a
    risk distribution rather than a pass/fail line. And every new hull type — giant container
    ships with vast deck cargo, wind-assisted vessels, autonomous ships with unfamiliar weight
    distribution — reopens the same two questions this chapter began with: does it float, and
    does it come back upright?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET ocean program criteria; Muckle &amp; Tupper, <em>Muckle's Naval Architecture</em>; Rawson &amp; Tupper, <em>Basic Ship Theory</em>.</p>

